const intro={"part":"ida","c":13,"slug":"canto-13","title":"Fierro and Cruz Cross the Frontier: The End of Part One","summary":"Fierro tells Cruz that the two men are alike in being pursued, and proposes seeking refuge beyond the frontier. He reflects on suffering, faith, the human powers of reason and speech, and the dangers of their proposed journey. He imagines that they may find shelter in an Indigenous encampment, an expectation shaped by his own prejudices and uncertain information. At the end, the singer breaks his guitar; the narration describes Fierro and Cruz taking a group of horses, crossing the frontier and disappearing into the open country. The poem closes without confirming what will become of them.","people":"Martín Fierro; Sergeant Cruz; an external closing narrator in the final stanzas; Indigenous people discussed through Fierro's speculation, rather than speaking for themselves.","themes":"Friendship in exile; crossing borders; imagined refuge and prejudice; faith and human dignity; the end of an oral song; uncertainty about what comes next.","words":"toldería (Indigenous settlement); cacique (Indigenous leader); tropilla (group of horses); ñandú (rhea); bolas or boleadoras (throwing device); guitarra (guitar).","questions":["Why does Fierro describe himself and Cruz as made from the same wood?","Which claims about life beyond the frontier are Fierro's expectations rather than verified facts?","What does breaking the guitar signify at the end of a poem that began with singing?","What information does the final narrator provide about the crossing, and what remains unknown?"],"nums":[367,389,392],"analysis":"The closing shift from Fierro's voice to a narrator reporting the journey is crucial. Fierro's imagined Indigenous refuge should not be mistaken for historical testimony about every Indigenous community. The 1872 ending is open: the fate of both men is withheld until the 1879 continuation. Even the journey is not a simple liberation, since the 